{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>I said [I shall not see Yah].</b> HaShem is not subject to occurrence, that the eye could perceive Him; rather, He is seen through His deeds. And the meaning is that he shall not see the deeds of HaShem in this world — that is, that he recognize the deeds of HaShem in this world — and this is 'in the land of the living.' It is as though he spells out his words, and behold, he first mentioned a person's pleasure in this world, which is that he recognized the deeds of HaShem, and afterwards, that he delight in the sight of the eye to be with his companions, and this is 'I shall look upon man no more.'" "<b>Hadal.</b> Like tevel. It is possible that the word is inverted, like kevesh / kesev, from the root of ḥaldoot <small>(Psalms 39:6)</small>. It is possible that it is like its parallel 'what is ḥadal I [the name of YHWH]'; and it is possible that this world is called ḥadal, for it ceases forever — for a person to be in it."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {0 "The bracketed [צ\"ל שהכיר] in the source marks a correction: read 'that he recognized' for the erroneous 'that he mentioned'; both readings preserved in sequence as the text stands."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:57:04.008174751Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}